{
  "dom_id": "step-q142984",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,{5}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(2,15),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(6,{2}^{2})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent and $Y=3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3},$ calculate $P(Y<43).$ \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,{5}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(2,15),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(6,{2}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =23 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot 15+{2}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 25+1\\cdot 15+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =256.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(23,256).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(Y<43).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<43) & =P(Z<\\frac{43-23}{\\sqrt{256}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.25)≈0.8944.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$P(Y<43)=0.8944.$",
